WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.010
- This notion that quality is given up

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.930
when you do a quantity of
creative is a huge misnomer

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.370
and something that I think would
really benefit the industry

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:11.922
and most of all the brands.

00:00:11.922 --> 00:00:14.130
(upbeat music)

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.593
You've got your perspective.

00:00:16.968 --> 00:00:19.235
(audience cheering)

00:00:19.235 --> 00:00:20.068
I just wanna be happy.

00:00:20.068 --> 00:00:21.040
Don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:25.071 --> 00:00:26.380
- So tell me.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:27.213
- Yes.

00:00:27.213 --> 00:00:30.492
- I've just had a very quick
briefing here from Harriet,

00:00:30.492 --> 00:00:32.990
but I gather you've got a little bit

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:35.603
of a different take on the market.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.120
- A little bit of a different take, yes.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.403
I think, you know, we started
the company 10 years ago.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.460
I, as an individual through my content

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.010
and things of that nature,
have been quite loud.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:49.450
But as an agency, we've
been incredibly quiet.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.460
Hence why I think we're kinda
ready to get a little louder.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.270
Hence why we made the
amazing addition of Harriet.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.910
- Was that a, um--

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:57.920
- Super deliberate.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.380
- Super deliberate being quiet?

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:00.213
- Yes.

00:01:00.213 --> 00:01:02.350
- What was the rational behind that?

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:03.940
- I think when you're disrupting,

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.360
you know, the longer
people underestimate you

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.683
or don't even know you
exist, tends to have value.

00:01:11.887 --> 00:01:14.930
I also was coming from the outside.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:18.190
I was coming from Silicon Valley investing

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:21.020
and wine ecommerce,
- Really?

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.150
- So I came from a very
different background, I, yes--

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.310
- Wine ecommerce?

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.710
- In 1996, I launched one

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.840
of the first ecommerce
wine businesses in America.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.580
So that was my first career,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.890
being a marketer for my
family's liquor business

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.640
and launching this big
ecommerce business was really

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.530
how I learned my craft of media

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.150
and creative and things of that nature.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:44.257
And so--

00:01:47.016 --> 00:01:49.351
- What made you decide to leave wine?

00:01:49.351 --> 00:01:51.213
I mean, personally I'd
have stuck with the wine.

00:01:51.213 --> 00:01:52.797
(both laugh)

00:01:52.797 --> 00:01:53.630
You know, what made--

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.480
- It was a family business--
- Okay.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:00.480
- And my, you know, I got
into my early and mid 30s

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.480
I'd invested in Facebook
and Twitter and Tumblr,

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.640
which obviously changed
the course of my career.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.160
The world, the world opened
up for me a little bit

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.600
and I started realizing
that I was a marketer.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.510
That I was a marketer that happened

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.920
to grow out a wine business.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.350
I was a marketer that
happened to understand

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:19.870
how to invest in apps 'cause I thought

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.840
they would be the platforms
that people would market on.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.090
So, there was a little
bit of a self-awareness,

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.515
self-revelation kind of going on for me.

00:02:26.515 --> 00:02:29.120
And then I also knew that
I didn't know anything

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.810
about the biggest companies in the world.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.330
That I had done the small business thing,

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.130
that I had done the Silicon Valley thing,

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:37.350
but I had never really understood

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:41.250
what Coca-Cola or Budweiser
or BMW was thinking

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.970
and, more importantly,
watching from the outside

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.190
I was curious to why they were
doing what they were doing.

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:51.420
So I decided to start the
agency for two reasons.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.740
One, I wanted to learn that world

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.248
and two, I wanted to
buy brands eventually.

00:02:58.248 --> 00:02:59.140
- Wow!

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:02.120
- So my big ambition
to be in this industry

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:06.237
is to buy nostalgic brands
from CBG Holding companies.

00:03:06.237 --> 00:03:07.070
- Okay.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:08.813
Wow!

00:03:08.813 --> 00:03:10.060
- Yeah, so a little bit
of a different angle.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:11.190
- Yeah! (laughs)

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.470
- And so that all
manifests, as you can tell,

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.730
in the form of creating
a very different shop.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.360
First of all, the first 30-40 employees

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.020
at VaynerMedia back in 2009

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.913
to 2011 had zero days
of agency experience.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.945
So that was interesting.

00:03:27.945 --> 00:03:30.480
Number two, I built an agency

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.023
that did creative and
media all under one house.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.950
Which now seems to be a conversation,

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:39.859
but was very foreign in 2009
when I started VaynerMedia.

00:03:39.859 --> 00:03:40.692
- Okay.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:45.029
How many people do you
got at your agency now?

00:03:45.029 --> 00:03:45.862
- Eight?

00:03:45.862 --> 00:03:46.695
- (Harriet) Eight, just over 800.

00:03:46.695 --> 00:03:48.260
- Okay, wow!

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.700
Big, I thought you were
going to say 40 or something.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.706
- You know, this is back
to kinda the strategy,

00:03:54.706 --> 00:03:58.500
you know, by being so quiet, you know,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.440
we've been able to sneak
up on our size, our scale.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.930
You know we have an office in London.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:07.930
We're opening up
Singapore this summer so--

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:10.890
- 800 people!

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:11.723
- Yes! (laughs)

00:04:11.723 --> 00:04:13.400
- Wow, I'm absolutely blown away

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.680
by that, that's a big agency.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.210
- Yeah, it's the biggest,
you know it's funny.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:19.590
I think when you walk around here and,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:22.660
Harriet maybe you can add some
context, it's interesting.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.960
You have a small group of people
who are either our clients,

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.020
which we have many of, or
people that have worked with us,

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.710
or people that have followed me on social

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:32.510
that have a bigger inclining

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.860
and probably think we're
the next big thing.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.940
And then you have an
enormous amount of people

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.300
walking around here that
still have never heard of us.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.430
And it's kind of fun to be an enigma.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:43.780
- Yeah, I can see that!

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.382
- But it's also fun to now
be at a maturity level where,

00:04:47.382 --> 00:04:50.120
when you're not only 800
people, when you're not only,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.760
you know, 150 million
dollar revenue business,

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:53.972
and you're an independent, I mean,

00:04:53.972 --> 00:04:56.960
I don't know the stats on Wieden+Kennedy,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.474
but we have to be the
largest independent agency

00:04:59.474 --> 00:05:03.770
in this industry besides Wieden and yet,

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:07.270
by not over emphasizing on press

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:09.370
and awards in our first decade,

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:12.910
it hasn't allowed the industry
to get to know us just yet.

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.996
Which will be fun to,
you know, be involved

00:05:15.996 --> 00:05:19.830
in the industry a little bit
more in this next decade.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.840
- Yeah, sorry you did
mention a couple stats there,

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.750
could you run those past me again?

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.630
You said you um--

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.130
- Our revenues are gonna
be 150 million this year.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.723
Our head count you just heard.

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:40.510
You know, we work with
Pepsi and Kraft and Chase.

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:41.779
Yes, Harriet?

00:05:41.779 --> 00:05:43.660
(muffled speaking)

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.450
I think you're right, it's funny.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.730
As such a showman, it's funny
that I'm more conservative

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:50.067
on the numbers I throw out than higher.

00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:50.900
- What was it?

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:51.733
- (Harriet) I think we track it

00:05:51.733 --> 00:05:53.382
to be a bit more than 150 million.

00:05:53.382 --> 00:05:56.530
- Plus, I'll put. (laughs)

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:59.420
You'd said you reckoned your ambition was

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:02.380
to buy nostalgic brands,
have you fulfilled that

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:03.770
or are you still heading towards it?

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:05.180
Is that your goal still?

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:08.070
- That's my goal, the only
thing I was probably very wrong

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:10.300
about when I started the
company 10 years ago was

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.330
that the the world would melt
financially five years ago,

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:15.700
five years later, that was my big thesis.

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:18.570
Let me learn the industry,
and then let me buy a brand

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:20.300
and convert my talent into the brand.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.410
- So you reckon that we were
gonna have a double dip.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.713
- I did, I though '09 with
the bailout was a bad decision

00:06:25.713 --> 00:06:28.430
and was putting a band aid

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:31.620
and my intuition was that
the world was gonna melt.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:34.297
My main thesis to start the industry was

00:06:34.297 --> 00:06:38.410
Facebook, Amazon, and
Netflix were gonna win

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:40.250
and the world was gonna melt.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:43.610
And I was right about
three of the four things.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:45.750
And the fourth one has led
to a very different path.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.880
I originally thought my
agency would be converted

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.410
into a brand.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:53.060
Now, it's too big of a company
and we enjoy it too much

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.730
that when I do buy a brand eventually

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:58.501
in three, five, seven, nine years, then,

00:06:58.501 --> 00:07:01.080
then we're gonna still
keep the agency obviously.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.031
And we're gonna take some
of the agency people over

00:07:03.031 --> 00:07:07.340
to work on the businesses,
but I just don't know when

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.870
that will be and honestly I'm so worried

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:12.650
about building the agency
that I'm not overly,

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:15.450
I can't control that anyway so I have to,

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:18.120
I have to be prepared, but
I don't spend a whole lot

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.320
of time trying to figure out when.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:21.720
- This is fascinating stuff.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:23.953
- Yes, it's definitely different.

00:07:23.953 --> 00:07:26.510
- Yeah, I'm really interested,
definitely interesting.

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:28.970
- I also, I also think
it's double interesting,

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:29.803
and let me tell you why.

00:07:29.803 --> 00:07:32.010
I'm not sure, but one of my
things that I'm very passionate

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.410
about is to inspire a bigger push

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.010
towards independent agencies
doing different things.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.120
I think it would be
healthy for the business,

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:42.540
I think the holding companies
are smart enough to know

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:44.950
what they need to worry
about is the consultancies.

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:47.470
Right, they're gonna worry
about Accenture and others.

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:50.090
But I do think for the
health of the industry,

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:53.240
having more significant independent shops

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.710
I think would really matter.

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:56.310
- Well, you see that in television.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:59.721
I mean the great, most of the
great kind of creative ideas

00:07:59.721 --> 00:08:02.170
on television and drama
and things are come,

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:05.137
certainly in the UK, coming
out of the Indie community.

00:08:05.137 --> 00:08:07.120
- They have more freedom.
- Yeah, you have more freedom!

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:08.810
- You know, I mean let's
call it what it is.

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:13.250
There's a very financially
heavy infrastructure to

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:16.400
this creative field, I mean--
- Yeah, yeah that's right too.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.670
- Most, you know, when you
look at all the creative shops

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:20.460
that have worked down there at the end,

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.779
if you follow all the
lines, they're run by a CFO.

00:08:22.779 --> 00:08:23.612
- Yeah.

00:08:23.612 --> 00:08:24.923
- They're not run by a CCO.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:26.730
- Yeah, that's true.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:27.563
- That is true.

00:08:27.563 --> 00:08:29.050
And I think it's something
that's worth having more

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:30.200
of a conversation about.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:31.033
- Yeah.

00:08:31.033 --> 00:08:34.620
So what would you like me to
focus on within my 300 words?

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.947
- That, that we need a more
progressive conversation

00:08:36.947 --> 00:08:39.090
on volume of creative,

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:42.539
that the industry needs to
start not demonizing quantity.

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:43.540
- Okay.

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:47.800
- This notion that quality is given up

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:51.710
when you do a quantity of
creative is a huge misnomer

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:54.130
and something that I think would
really benefit the industry

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:55.667
and most of all the brands.

00:08:55.667 --> 00:08:57.460
- And how and what way would it benefit?

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:00.110
- Most brands can't make relevant creative

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:01.917
because they're not making enough creative

00:09:01.917 --> 00:09:04.260
to hit the many different cohorts

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:05.770
they need to be talking to.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:08.823
And so they end up making
one vanilla piece of video.

00:09:10.191 --> 00:09:11.860
- I know, I kinda see what you mean.

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:12.693
Don't speak 'cause I want

00:09:12.693 --> 00:09:14.160
to just get this down--
- You got it.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.357
- In your great words.

00:09:15.357 --> 00:09:17.667
I'm sorry, "They're not
making enough creative

00:09:17.667 --> 00:09:19.543
"to hit all the cohorts,

00:09:20.779 --> 00:09:23.443
"so just making one vanilla
piece of work," okay.

00:09:23.443 --> 00:09:28.370
- You know, we are too
infatuated with reach

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:31.050
and not enough around relevance.

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:35.410
And if you don't make 70, 400, 3000 pieces

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:36.380
of meaningful content,

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:38.210
you can never create actual relevance.

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:39.180
- Yeah, okay.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:41.600
- But if you go walk
the streets right now,

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.720
one of the worst words
in the creative community

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:45.190
is volume and quantity.

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:46.200
- That's very true, yeah.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:47.180
- And that needs to be cut,

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:49.860
we need to have a more thoughtful
conversation around it.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.170
- Yeah, they're bad words at the moment.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:53.380
- They are, and so--

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:57.630
- Yeah, funny enough I was
at a Net, um, a Net, no god,

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.510
an HBO session yesterday
where they were saying,

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.067
"Oh, how can you increase your output

00:10:02.067 --> 00:10:04.327
"without, sort of, losing the secret sauce

00:10:04.327 --> 00:10:05.837
"of HBO, you know, volume,

00:10:05.837 --> 00:10:08.334
"and how can you do that
without sacrificing--"

00:10:08.334 --> 00:10:10.420
- Now, now that in the form

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:13.510
of a one hour drama is a
far more interesting debate

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:16.940
than trying to communicate
a toothpaste or a shampoo

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:19.880
or a soda pop to the end consumer, right?

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:22.860
You know, if I'm trying
to sell, what we know

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:26.700
about long-form video,
theater, movies, television

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:29.190
is some shows are not meant for everybody.

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.560
We have four people here and that's okay.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.947
Meanwhile, we have this
audacious conversation

00:10:32.947 --> 00:10:34.920
that this 30 second spot is supposed

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:38.080
to make everybody inspired to buy.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:39.473
- That's very true.

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:42.400
- HBO does do a lot of creative.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:43.233
- Yeah, they do.

00:10:43.233 --> 00:10:44.950
- They have many different shows.

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:48.720
If HBO was a creative agency,
they would have one show.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.233
- Yeah, I love that, (laughs) yeah, yeah.

00:10:56.299 --> 00:10:57.600
Yes, so that's a very important--

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:58.937
- Here's another one,
here's one of the report,

00:10:58.937 --> 00:11:01.230
and I know it's limited words,
but you do whatever you do,

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:03.570
I'm gonna give you one more
point that's big for us.

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:07.853
We are outrageously structured
on actual business results.

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:13.780
Because my selfish ambition
is to own businesses,

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:16.110
all the work we're doing is
actually trying to figure out

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:19.803
how to drive businesses,
not maximize our revenue.

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:25.913
And that has led us to
being more client-centric.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:30.300
Because I don't want to
necessarily be the most profitable,

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:33.563
I wanna figure out if
my hypotheses are true.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:36.473
